<a name="tdslimitations"/><h1>Limitations</h1>

<p>The implementation of the TDS protocol is likely sufficient for most applications, but it isn't 100% complete.  Some notable limitations follow:</p>

<ul>
  <li>TLS/SSL mutual authentication requires a client that can present a client certificate.  Not all clients can (eg. FreeTDS).  See <a href="tdsprototls.html">TLS/SSL Encryption and Authentication</a>.</li>
  <li>For odbc-backed instances, the connect string needs mars=yes for cursors and positioned updates to work.  Without it, a query sent while another cursor still has rows pending fails with "Connection is busy with results for another command".  See the <a href="../configreference.html#odbc">SQL Relay Configuration Reference</a> for the <i>mars</i> option.</li>
  <li>Only TDS 5.0 and TDS 7.0 or later clients can log in.  Older clients are refused, even though <i>tdsversion</i> accepts 420.  TDS 5.0 clients that mix byte orders are also refused.</li>
  <li>SAP's own password encryption (eg. Open Client's CS_SEC_ENCRYPTION) requires SAP's client libraries to be installed on the SQL Relay host.  Without them, clients are asked to log in again without password encryption.  This is unrelated to TLS/SSL encryption.</li>
  <li>Bulk copy is supported, but each row is inserted separately, so it is no faster than individual inserts.  It isn't supported for TDS 5.0 clients, or when a client sends it as a remote procedure call.</li>
  <li>Positioned updates and deletes through a client-side cursor can only reach the first 1024 rows of a multi-row fetch.  Past that, they fail with "The cursor is not on a row that can be updated".  WHERE CURRENT OF queries aren't affected.</li>
  <li>TDS 5.0 clients can't use the sp_cursorprepare, sp_cursoropen, sp_cursorexecute, sp_cursorprepexec or sp_cursorfetch procedures, or receive extremely wide result sets (more than 65535 columns).
----</li>
</ul>

